  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 AM MDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nUpper high fixed over western New Mexico today will continue the \nweek-long trend of above normal temperatures. Lowland highs will \nagain flirt with the triple-digit mark, generally in the 96-102 \nrange. This is 5-8 degrees warmer than usual for the end of \nAugust. Not quite record setting, but still solidly above normal.\n\nAnother round of isolated (20-30% coverage lowlands, 40-60% \ncoverage mountains) showers and thunderstorms are expected this\nafternoon. Blend of NBM and CONSMOS guidance used for short-term \nPoPs this forecast, looking to ensure at least mentionable storm\nchances are included for much of southern New Mexico this\nafternoon and western New Mexico tomorrow. Some marginal moisture\nremains in place across southern New Mexico and far west Texas, \nindicated by morning surface dewpoints in the lower 50s and \nprecipitable water values in the 1.0-1.2\" range. This will be \nsufficient for terrain forcing to initiate convection over the \nhigh elevations by 12-1 PM. \n\nMain concern early this afternoon will be flash flooding over the\nSacramento Mountains. Recent rainfall there (widespread \n0.50-1.50\" along the western ridge, with locally higher amounts \npast 48 hours) will lead to quicker runoff and canyon/creek \nflooding with any storms that form today. Will be a bit more\nliberal with flood products there today.\n\nElsewhere, isolated, hit-and-miss (mostly miss) storms will pose \na risk of brief downpours and gusty outflow winds this afternoon. \nHRRR signals that rain chances will end quickly tonight, likely \naround sunset.\n\nUpper high flattens and begins a subtle progression eastward over\nthe weekend. This will focus better moisture further west across\neastern Arizona and western New Mexico, which will be the best\nchances for storms Saturday/Sunday. Isolated storms along and west\nthe Continental Divide (Grant and Hidalgo Counties for our \nforecast area) will be capable of gusty winds and blowing dust.\nAdded a 4-8 PM Sat/Sun mention of dust for Lordsburg and the I-10\ncorridor near the State Line due to sensitivity. Most of the Rio \nGrande/Tularosa basins look to be dry and warm.\n\nWeather pattern looks favorable to reintroduce widespread rain \nchances in early September, with an upper high over the \nSoutheastern U.S. and weak southerly flow aloft over our region\nallowing for moisture flow. ECMWF runs have been less bullish on\nmoisture, keeping an elongated upper high fixed over most of the \nSouthern US. NBM leans hard on ECMWF long term, so the extended \nforecast will have very low PoPs (5-10%). I do think those chances\nwill increase as we head into next week. Expecting more isolated \n(low but at least mentionable) showers and thunderstorms chances \nnext week, instead of the dry forecast currently in place.",
